{
 "about": "Ruleset versions: a name for each hash of the rule files (pqlite/version.py RULESET_FILES). A run records ruleset_version and ruleset_sha256 in receipt.json. Each version lists sha256, since (the first tool version that shipped it), verdict_impact (yes when the same trees can get a different verdict, check status or count than under the previous ruleset; no when only comments, wording or code layout changed) and files (path and sha256 of each rule file). The file list can differ between versions: ruleset 1 hashed six files, ruleset 2 hashes eleven: it adds scan/skip-dirs, scan/profiles.json, pqlite/rules.py, pqlite/skipdirs.py and pqlite/tlsprobe.py. Ruleset 3 hashes fourteen: it adds pqlite/impl.py, scan/kat-kpqc.json and scan/kat (the reference KAT excerpts, one digest over the *.json files of the folder, pqlite/version.py path_sha256). Ruleset 4 hashes the same fourteen and counts SLH-DSA 192 and 256 (FIPS 205) as an expected signature (E-SLHDSA) and blocks SLH-DSA 128 as below category 3. --verify recomputes a receipt's ruleset_sha256 from the files of the ruleset it names. Changing a rule file needs a new name here, a new current, and a CHANGELOG entry with its verdict impact. A ruleset with verdict_impact yes first ships in a MINOR release (MAJOR from 1.0), never a PATCH release (tests/version_policy.py). pqlite/version.py --ruleset-json prints sha256 and files for the rule files on disk. Ruleset 5 hashes the same fourteen files with the same rules as ruleset 4; only comments changed. From ruleset 5 a record also keeps comment_free (the hash of the rule files without comments) and aliases: a later edit that changes only comments is recorded as an alias of the same name (pqlite/version.py --alias), so comments no longer rename a ruleset. --verify accepts the recorded hash or an alias. Ruleset 6 hashes the same fourteen files: one awk mask (MASKFN, the same text in scan/cbom-scan.sh and scan/residue.sh) hides the same values as prove.py REDACT, residue.sh masks its excerpts too, and both add ssh-keygen -N. Verdict impact yes only for a file over MAX_FILE_MIB, where prove falls back to the residue.sh excerpt and a masked secret value could hold a rule word. Ruleset 7 hashes the same fourteen files: a classical row whose stated size is below 112-bit security (RSA, DSA, DH or ElGamal under 2048 bits, a curve under 224 bits) is never allowed (NIS draft cryptographic security guideline, November 2026); PQ/PQ hybrid lines are marked; allowlist entries read residual_risk, compensating_control and replacement_plan; tls_probe 0.5.0 reports the cipher suite and the certificate expiry. Verdict impact yes only for such a weak classical key that an allow rule, a hybrid or an allowlist entry used to take out of the blocking count. Ruleset 8 hashes the same fourteen files: a TLS target without a CA is verified against the system trust store and its host name (tls_probe 0.6.0), trust on first use needs an explicit #tofu, and a handshake that fails certificate verification leaves b-tls undecided instead of counting as a hybrid result or a refusal. Verdict impact yes only for TLS targets: one given without a CA whose certificate the system trust store does not accept (a self-signed or private CA) moves from pass to undecided, and one whose certificate does not verify against its CA file moves from fail to undecided.",
 "current": "8",
